    "ls|LS17_S2_U_273": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 273\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH NOVEMBER, 2019/ KARTIKA 28, 1941 (SAKA)\n CHALLANING OF VEHICLES\n †273.  SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT\nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of vehicles belonging to Government and non-Government\noffices challaned after implementation of new traffic rules till date and the\namount of revenue earned from it;\n(b) whether maintenance and fitness certificates are obtained for Government\nvehicles in order to overcome its problems from time to time; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS\n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI)\n(a)   to  (c):    The information is being collected and will be laid on the Table\nof the House.\n******",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_274": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 274\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 6TH DECEMBER, 2019\nTRAUMA CENTRES\n†*274. DR. DHAL SINGH BISEN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether trauma centres have been set up in the State of Madhya Pradesh in\ncollaboration with the Union Government;\n(b) if so, the locations/districts where the trauma centres have been opened/set up\nincluding the dates on which these centres were opened;\n(c) whether all the centres are functional at present and adequate number of doctors\nare working in these centres and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the time by which these centres are likely to\nbecome functional; and\n(e) whether the Union Government is likely to give permission for recruitment of\ndoctors under the National Health Mission for these trauma centres so as to ensure\nsmooth functioning of these centres?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 274* FOR 6TH DECEMBER, 2019\n(a) & (b) Yes. Under the scheme “Assistance for Capacity Building for\nDeveloping Trauma Care Facilities in Government Hospitals on National\nHighways”, establishing of 5(five) Trauma Care Facilities w as approved during the\n11th Five Year Plan in the State of Madhya Pradesh one each at Civil Hospital,\nShivpuri; Gajra Raja Medical College (GRMC) Hospital, Gwalior; District\nHospital, Narsimhapur; District Hospital, Sagar and District Hospital, Seoni. As per\nthe information made ava ilable by the State Government, the said Trauma Care\nFacilities were opened during the year as indicated below:\nTrauma Care Facility Year\nCivil Hospital, Shivpuri 2008\nGajra Raja Medical College (GRMC) Hospital, Gwalior 2010\nDistrict Hospital, Narsimhapur 2017\nDistrict Hospital, Sagar 2017\nDistrict Hospital, Seoni 2017\n(c) & d) As reported by the State Government, the posts of doctors have been\nsanctioned as per the guidelines of the programme namely “Assistance for Capacity\nBuilding for Developing Trauma Care Facilities in Government Hospitals on\nNational Highways”. However, recruitment is a continuous process and is carried\nout by State Government as and when vacancies occur.  As per information made\navailable by the State Government, a ll five Trauma Care facilities are functional\nwith the existing manpower/ staff.\n(e) Under National Health Mission (NHM), financial and technical support is\nprovided to States /UTs for Trauma Centres to be established till District Hospital\nlevel if proposed in the Programme Implementation Plans (PIPs) of the State/ UTs.\nThe support for Human Resources for trauma centres till District level is provided\nunder NHM as per need which is decided jointly by the States and Centre during\nNPCC (National Programme Co -ordination Committee) meeting of the concerned\nState/ UT. Manpower is provided to trauma centre at District Hospitals are as per\nthe norms Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS).",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_275": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *275 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.12.2019 \n \nAlternatives to Plastic \n \n*275. SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any steps to ban/curb/minimize the use of plastic; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the available alternatives to plastic for packaging \nof various products; and \n(c) the details of the schemes formulated by the Government to minimize the use of \nplastic and promote alternatives to it along with the funds allocated for the purpose, \nscheme-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 275 \nto be answered on Friday, the 6th December, 2019 on “Alternatives to Plastic” by Shri \nSanjay Kaka Patil \n \n(a)  to (c) The Government has notified Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016. The \nRules prohibits the use of carry bag made of virgin or recycled plastic less than fifty microns \nin thickness. Sachets made of plastic material for storing, packing or selling gutkha, tobacco \nand pan masala is also prohibited. As per the Rules, the generators of waste have been \nmandated to take steps to minimize generation of plastic waste, not to litter the plastic waste, \nensure segregated storage of waste at source and handover segregated waste to local bodies or \nagencies authorized by the local bodies. The rules also mandate the responsibilities of local \nbodies, gram panchayats, waste generators, retailers and street vendors to manage plastic \nwaste. The rules mandate the producers, importers and brand owners to work out modalities \nfor waste collection system based on the principle of Extended Producer Responsibility. \n \nCentral Pollution Control Board as developed guidelines for (i) Management of Sanitary \nWaste (ii) Disposal of non-recyclable fraction (Multi-layered) plastic waste (iii) Collection, \nSegregation and Disposal of Plastic Waste (iv) Co-processing of plastic waste in Cement \nKilns (v) Disposal of Thermoset Plastic Waste including Sheet Moulding Compound (SMC) \nand Fibre Reinforced plastic \n \nFurther, Hon’ble Prime Minister has announced India’s pledge to phase out all Single-use \nplastic by 2022. In this perspective, to create mass awareness among the common public, \nplastic industry, recycling units and other stakeholders, a three-phase campaign of “Swachhta \nHi Sewa” was launched by Hon'ble Prime Minister from 11th Sep, 2019, which ended on \nDiwali i.e. 27th October 2019. This campaign inter-alia focused on awareness, advocacy, \ncollection and safe disposal of littered plastic. \n \nThere is no specific scheme formulated by the Government, however, in view to minimize \nthe usage of plastics, the Ministry has issued “Standard Guidelines on Single Use Plastic” \nwith suggested different set of actions for State/UT Government to minimize the usage of \nsingle use plastics. The guidelines are focused to target those kind of Single Use Plastic for \nwhich alternatives are readily available. Such as jute bags, cloth bags, paper bags are \navailable as an alternative to plastic carry bags. Similarly, sufficient alternatives available for \nthe plastic cutlery like paper plates and leaf plates, paper/earthen cups and glass, wooden \nspoons, paper straws, multi-layered (aluminum + paper) pouches in place of container and \ncontainer lids, wooden trays, stirrers made of wood. Apart from the traditional material, the \nnew material like bagasse based products, banana stem based products are widely available in \nmarket. \n \n Further, the Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016 defines the norms for compostable plastic \nas an ecologically sustainable and green alternatives of plastics. The provision of thickness is \nnot applicable to carry bags made up of compostable plastic. However, the compostable \nplastics has to conform to the Indian Standard: IS 17088:2008 titled as Specifications for \nCompostable Plastics. Certificate has been granted to 53 compostable plastic bags \nmanufacturers/sellers till now. \n***** ",
