    "ls|LS17_S2_U_312": " \n \nO.I.H. \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 312 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 10.12.2019 \n \nTARGET UNDER PMAY \n \n312. SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether many States are lagging behind in terms  of the target set to provide maximum \nbenefit to the people residing in rural areas of th e country under the Pradhan Mantri \nAwas Yojana (PMAY) during the last five years; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise; \n(c) whether the pace of work is slow in the North-E astern States, hilly areas and tribal areas \nof the country under the said scheme; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof, State-wise?         \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n                                            (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the table of the house. \n \n******* \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA  STARRED QUESTION \nNO.* 312 (12 TH  POSITION) TO BE ANSWERED ON 10.12.2019 REGARDING \nTARGETS UNDER PMAY \n(a) to (d):   To achieve the objective of “Housing for All” by 2022, in rural areas of the country, \nthe Government has re-structured the erstwhile rura l housing scheme Indira Awaas Yojana \n(IAY) into Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana- Gramin (PMAY-G) w.e.f. 1 \nst  April, 2016.  \nThe target under PMAY-G is construction of 2.95 cro re houses by year 2021-22 in two \nphases. Under Phase-I (2016-17 to 2018-19), against  the target of 1 crore houses, 97.9 lakh \nhouses have been sanctioned and 85.7 lakh houses ha ve been completed, as on 06.12.2019. The \ntarget for Phase II (2019-20 to 2021-22) is constru ction of 1.95 crore houses. In 2019-20, target \nof 51,05,396 houses have been allocated to the Stat es/UTs out of which 39,03,140 beneficiaries \nhave been given sanctions and 2,56,852 houses have been completed as on 06.12.2019. As per \nthe Framework for Implementation of PMAY-G, the tim e given for construction of PMAY-G \nhouse is 12 months from the date of sanction.  \nThe State/UT-wise details (including North-Eastern States and Hilly States/UTs) of \ntargets allocated and achievements thereof during 2016-17 to 2019-20 are given in  Annexure . \n******* \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF LOK SABHA  STARRED QUESTION \nNO.*312 (12 \nTH  POSITION) TO BE ANSWERED ON 10.12.2019 REGARDING \nTARGETS UNDER PMAY \nState/UT wise Targets and completion of houses duri ng Phase I and Phase II under Pradhan \nMantri Awaas Yojana- Gramin, as on 06.12.2019 \n                                                                                                                         \n(Units in number)  \nS. \nNo. \n  \nState \n  \nPhase I (2016-17 to 2018-19) \n Phase II (2019-20) \nHouse \nTargeted \nHouse \nCompleted \nHouse \nTargeted \nHouse \nCompleted \n1 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 11221 94 7500 0 \n2 ASSAM 259814 194665 200000 660 \n3 BIHAR 1176617 719656 800000 16848 \n4 CHHATTISGARH 788235 728052 151100 61 \n5 GOA 427 25 0 0 \n6 GUJARAT 204703 186574 107100 4149 \n7 HARYANA 21502 14409 0 0 \n8 HIMACHAL PRADESH 7385 6675 900 42 \n9 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 38772 17598 52500 10 \n10 JHARKHAND 528791 476439 322000 17975 \n11 KERALA 42431 16386 0 0 \n12 MADHYA PRADESH 1403593 1328595 830100 57421 \n13 MAHARASHTRA 449820 362754 289700 9919 \n14 MANIPUR 9740 8309 8900 0 \n15 MEGHALAYA 20745 14560 17200 0 \n16 MIZORAM 6600 2260 1500 0 \n17 NAGALAND 8481 350 5900 0 \n18 ODISHA 992558 860687 565000 70801 \n19 PUNJAB 14000 13551 10000 0 \n20 RAJASTHAN 687091 654007 364000 16369 \n21 SIKKIM 1079 1033 0 0 \n22 TAMIL NADU 327552 201878 200000 1101 \n23 TRIPURA 24989 24251 28838 292 \n24 UTTAR PRADESH 1282616 1255445 178900 59838 \n25 UTTARAKHAND 15776 12306 0 0 \n26 WEST BENGAL 1397474 1342112 830000 1343 \n27 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 972 193 400 23 \n28 DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI  7605 409 0 0 \n29 DAMAN AND DIU 15 13 0 0 \n30 LAKSHADWEEP 57 3 58 0 \n31 PUDUCHERRY 0 0 0 0 \n32 ANDHRA PRADESH 123112 46718 47800 0 \n33 KARNATAKA 145349 79547 86000 0 \n34 TELANGANA 0 0 0 0 \n35 LADAKH 0 955 0 0 \n Total 9999122 8570509 5105396  256852 \n\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_313": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES & PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 313 \nANSWERED ON 19.11.2019 \n \nFAME INDIA Scheme \n \n313. MS. RAMYA HARIDAS: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES AND PUBLIC ENTERPRISES भारी उद्योग \nएवं ऱोक उद्यम मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has  withdrawn the incentives given to buyers  of mild hybrid \nvehicles under Faster  Adoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid  and Electric Vehicles in \nIndia (FAME INDIA) scheme; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the said scheme was launched in 2015 to popularize the use of electric vehicles \nin the country; and \n \n(d) if so, the total number of buyers  of electric vehicles given cash and non -cash subsidies \nsince the launch of the scheme? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES & PUBLIC ENTERPRISES \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n********  \n \n(a) &(b):   Yes Sir,  benefit of incentives to buyers of mild hybrid vehicles under Faster \nAdoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid and Electric Vehicles in (FAME -INDIA) scheme \nwas withdrawn with  effect from 1 stApril, 2017 based on the third party evaluation of \nPhase-I of FAME India Scheme. \n \n(c) &(d):  The Phase-I of the FAME India Scheme was launched in 2015 and continued up  \nto 31 st March, 2019. Based on outcome and experience gained during the Phase -I of \nFAME India Scheme and after having consultations with all stakeholders including \nIndustry and Industry Associations, the second phase of FAME India Scheme has been \ncommenced from 01 st April, 2019 for a period of 3 years. As on date approx. 2.85 lakhs \nbuyers of electric/hybrid vehicles (xEVs) have been supported by subsidy of Rs. 360 crore \n(approx.) under the FAME India Scheme. \n \n*****  ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_314": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n314\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19\nTH \n \nNOVEMBER, 2019\n \n \nPREMIUM COLLECTION UNDER PMFBY\n \n \n314. SHRI LAVU SRI KRISHNA DEVARAYALU: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe details of gross premium collected by insurance companies under PM Fasal Bima \nYojana since its implementation, season\n-\nwise, year\n-\nwise, company\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether it is true that the number of insured farmers are either constant or have\n \ndeclined if one looks at year\n-\non\n-\nyear basis; \n \n \n(c)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether it has come to the notice of the Ministry that there are many instances where \ncrop insurance companies are charging very high premium a\nnd thereby hugely burdening the \nStates; \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, whether there are any proposal before the Ministry to put a ceiling on crop \ninsurance premium charged by insurance companies, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(f)\n \n \nwhether\n \nthere are any plans to amend the PM Fasal Bima Yojana guidelines, if so, \nthe details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(\na):\n \nSeason\n-\nwise, year\n-\nwise, Company\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise premium collected by \ninsurance companies under Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) since inception of \nthe scheme \nare at\n \nAnenxure\n.\n \n \n(b) & (c)\n:\n \nNumber of farmer applicants has increased from 20% under erstwhile crop \ninsurance schemes to 30% under PMFBY.   However, 2017\n-\n18 and 2018\n-\n19 being good \nmonsoon years, pan\n-\nIndia coverage under the scheme is almost constant inspite the fact that \ntwo major St\na\nt\nes namely, Bihar and West\n \nBengal had opted out of the\n \nscheme.\n \n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n \n\n \n \n-\n \n2 \n-\n \n \n \n(d)\n:\n \nActuarial/bidd\ned premium rates have been char\nged by the insurance companies under \nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) and Restructured Weather Based Crop \nInsur\nance Scheme (RWBCIS),  but farmers have to pay a low flat premium rate of maximum \nof 1.5% for Rabi, 2% for Kharif food and Oilseed crops and 5% for commercial/horticultural \ncrop.  As per insurance principles, where yield variability is relatively less and \nlow claims \nhave been paid in the past, actuarial premium rates are comparatively very low like in \nUttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh etc., whereas in States like Gujarat, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, \nRajasthan, Maharashtra etc. where yield variability is more and hig\nher claims have been paid \nin the\n \npast, the actuarial premium rates are increased. \n \n \n(e):\n \nNo Sir.\n \n \n(f)\n:\n \nThe amendment/revisions in the crop insurance schemes is\n \na continuous process and \ndecisions on it are taken from time to time after consultation with various stakeholders\n.\n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n \nSeason\n-\nwise, Company\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise details of Premium collected by insurance companies since inception of the \nscheme till Rabi 2018\n-\n19 under PMFBY/RWBCIS\n \n \nState/UT Name \n \n \nSeason \n \n \nCompany \n \n \nFarmer \nPremium \n \n \nGOI Share in \nPremium \n \n \nState Share \nin Premium \n \n \nGross \nPremium \n \n \nRs in Crore \n \nA & N Islands\n \n2016\n-\n17 Rabi\n \nReliance\n \n \n           \n0.002 \n \n           \n0.005 \n \n           \n0.009 \n \n           \n0.016 \n \nA & N Islands\n \n2016\n-\n17 Rabi Total\n \n \n \n           \n0.002 \n \n           \n0.005 \n \n           \n0.009 \n \n           \n0.016 \n \nA & N Isl