 have improved from 38.8 in 2OOO to 3O.3 in 2O{9.\nThus, the country has shown consistent improvement over the years.\n....2t-",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_321": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.321 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH NOVEMBER, 2019 \nCulture Fishing and Fishing Assistance \n321.  SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \nWill the Minister of ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING AND FISHERIES मत्स्यपालन, \nपशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) the steps taken to certify fishermen under Recognition of Prior Learning Scheme; \n(b) the number of trainees enrolled by Agriculture Skill Council of India for the segments of \n‘Culture Fishing’ and ‘Fishing Assistance’ in the country including Andhra Pradesh; \n(c) the placement statistics of such trainees; \n(d) whether such training in the fishery segment also includes the training of disaster \nmanagement; and  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY  AND \nDAIRYING  \n(SHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI) \n(a):  Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL)  for people engaged in Fishery Sector is being \nundertaken under Pradhan Mantri K aushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY ) and State funded skill \nprogrammes. \n(b):  The total No. of trainees enrolled for RPL by various affiliated Training Providers is as \nunder; \nSegment Enrolled \nCulture Fishing ( Aquaculture Worker, Shrimp \nFarmer and Hatchery Production Worker) 4,445 \nCapture Fishing (Inland capture fisherman cum \nprimary processor, Marine capture fisherman \ncum primary processor) 15,816 \nFishing Assistance RPL  yet to be taken up \nTotal 20,261 \n \n(c):  The trainee enrolled in Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) Programme are already \nengaged in their respective occupations and no fresh placement is undertaken for them. \n(d) and (e): No Sir.  The Qualification Packs developed cover the skills required for an \nindividual to perform a specific task.  Disaster Management capsule is not included in the \nQualification Packs currently in use. \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_322": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF EX-SERVICEMEN WELFARE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11th December, 2019 \n \nWELFARE OF EX-SERVICEMEN \n*322. DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE j{kk ea=h  \nbe pleased to state: \n (a) the details of welfare  and rehabilitation schemes and financial assistance provided \nto ex-servicemen by the Government through various agencies; \n(b) the total amount distributed  for the welfare of ex -servicemen under  Raksha \nMantri’s Discretionary Fund  (RMDF) and the number of beneficiaries  of the Fund \nduring the last three years, year-wise; \n(c) the main activities of Kendriya Sainik Board and the manner in which the activities \nare beneficial to ex-servicemen and their families; and \n(d) the efforts made to activate Grievances Redressal Mechanism for ex-servicemen \nand to expedite the redressal of their grievances? \n \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                    (SHRI RAJNATH SINGH) \n      j{kk ea=h                  ¼Jh राजनाथ  स िंह  ) \n \n \n(a) to (d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n******* \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 322 FOR ANSWER ON 11.12.2019 \nREGARDING ‘WELFARE OF EX-SERVICEMEN’.  \n(a) The Welfare, Rehabilitation Scheme and Financial Assistance is provided by the \nGovernment through  Kendriya Sainik Board  (KSB), Directorate General Resettlement  \n(DGR) and the Service Headquarters. In addition various State Governments also have \nseparate schemes for welfare of Ex-servicemen (ESM).     \nDetails of Schemes run by KSB are at Annexure- A \n \nDetails of Rehabilitation schemes run by DGR are at Annexure –B \n \nDetails of measures instituted by Service Headquarters are at Annexure- C \n \n(b)  The nomenclature of Raksha Mantri’s Discretionary Fund (RMDF) has been \nchanged to Raksha Mantri’s Ex -Servicemen Welfare Fund (RMEWF). The details of \ntotal amount distributed along with number of beneficiaries for the welfare of Ex -\nservicemen under Raksha Mantri Ex-Servicemen Welfare Fund (RMEWF) during last  \nthree years  is as under :- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n (c) Details of the main activities of Kendriya Sainik Board are  placed at Annexure- D \n \n All the above activities are primarily for the welfare of Ex -servicemen and their \ndependents  to  lead a dignified life after retirement. It may be seen that the support \ncreated through the various schemes like penury grant, daughters’ marriage grant, \neducation grant, disabled children grant, house repair grant, medical treatment grant , \nserious disease grant, orphan grant etc have resulted in more compassionate support to \nthe families and children of ex -servicemen.  The educational opportunities of ex -\nservicemen’s children have been expanded.  A growing involvement of civil society and \nthe private sec tor with ex -servicemen’s welfare has resulted in improved mobilization \nof welfare funds. \n \n \n...2/- \nFinancial \nYear \nNo of \nbeneficiaries \nTotal Amount \nDisbursed \nRs (In Crores) \n2016-17 37,572 67.21 \n \n2017-18 29,303 57.81 \n \n2018-19 \n \n24,066 64.40 \n \nTotal  90,941 189.42 \n\n: 2 : \n \n A total of 90941 ESMs and their dependents have been benefited under these \nschemes during last three years (from 2016-17 to 2018-19). \n \n \n(d) Redressal o f  grievances is a key function of the Department of Ex -Servicemen \nWelfare. In the direction of prompt and satisfactory resolution of grievances, the \nfollowing initiatives were taken subsequent to 2014 :-  \n \n(i) Personal contact with petitioners  : Apart from written communications, \npetitioners are contacted over phone to obtain specifics of the grievance \nincluding service/pensioners’ details, Feedback about resolution of grievances  \nand  to ascertain level of satisfaction.  \n(ii) Categorization of grievances: The department has categorized the grievances \nin 27 categories according to the nature of grievances received from ex -\nservicemen to know the cause of the grievances and issuing instructions to the \nconcerned offices for redressal. \n(iii) Periodical monitoring at higher level: The status of pending pension \ngrievances is being monitored at the highest level of the department by the \nSecretary in regular weekly meetings. \n(iv) Synergy of the existing infrastructure: The existing set up of Sainik Boards \nis being u tilized for assisting the petitioners and other ex -servicemen for \ncompletion of documents/Records required for release of pension and assisting \nin State Govt. related issues/grievances. \nOverall percentage of disposal of grievances is 97% and the data are a vailable \nin the pgportal.gov.in. This has been achieved due to intense follow up, regular \ninteraction with the concerned offices as well as weekly review.  The impact of the \nabove indicated initiatives, together with strict compliance with shorter timeline of \n30 days for resolution of grievances and use of technology has resulted in \nreduction of average disposal time from 87 days in 2014 to 25 days in 2018 and \nfurther reduced to 21 days in 2019. \nThe efforts made above underscore our concerted efforts to redu ce grievances, \nbring down the disposal time, and promote dialogue and interaction with our ex -\nservicemen as also to sensitize the Rajya Sainik Boards (RSBs) Zila Sainik Boards \n(ZSBs) for resolving the grievances of ESM. \n \n***** \n\nANNEXURE-A REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322 FOR ANSWER ON 11.12.2019 REGARDING \n‘WELFARE OF EX-SERVICEMEN’.  \nDETAILS OF VARIOUS WELFARE SCHEMES PROVIDED TO ESM \n/WIDOWS \nBY KENDRIYA SAINIK BOARD (KSB)  \n \n \n1. Benefits given from Armed Forces Flag Day Fund under RMEWF :- \n \nS No. Grants Amount(in Rs) \n(a) Penury Grant (65 Yrs and above) \n(Non-Pensioners uptoHav Rank) \nRs 4,000/-pm         \n(Life time) \nWef 01 Apr 17 \n(b) Education Grant (upto two children) \n(i)  Boys/Girls upto Graduation \n(ii) Widows for PG \n(Pensioner/Non Pensioner upto  Hav Rank) \nand upto two children \n \nRs 1,000/-pm \n(c) Officer Cadet Grant (for Cadets of NDA \nonly) \nPensioner/Non-Pen upto Hav Rank) \n \nRs 1,000/-pm \n(d) Disabled Children Grant \n(Pensioner/Non-pen upto Hav Rank) \n \nRs 1,000/-pm \n(e) House Repair Grant \n(Pensioner/Non-Pen upto Hav Rank) \n 100% Disabled ESM \n Orphan Daughter (of all ranks) \n \nRs 20,000/- \n(One time) \n(f) Daughter’s Marriage Grant                       \n(upto 02 Daughters) \n(Pensioner/Non-Pen upto Hav Rank) \n \n \nRs 50,000/- * \nWidow Re-Marriage Grant \n(Pensioner/Non-Pen upto Hav Rank) \n*  If married solemnly on or after 21 Apr 16.  \n(g) Funeral Grant \n(Pensioner/Non-Pen upto Hav Rank) \n \nRs 5,000/- \n(h) Medical Treatment Grant \n(Non-pensioner upto Hav Rank) \n \nRs 30,000/- (Max) \n \n \n...2/- \n \n\n: 2 : \n \n(j) Orphan Grant \n(Pensioner/Non-pen All Ranks) \n Daughters of ex -servicemen till she is \nmarried. \n One Son of ex-servicemen upto 21 years \nof age. \n \nRs 1,000/-pm \n(k) Vocational Trg Grant For Widows \n(Pensioner/Non-Pen upto Hav Rank) \nRs 20,000/- \n(One Time) \n \n2. Modified Scooter Grant. Rs 57,500/ - provided to those ESM, who are \ndisabled after service with a disability of 50% or more and who are not covered under \nthe scheme of AG’s Branch of IHQ (Army, Navy & Air Force). \n \n3.   Serious diseases Grant from AFFD Fund to Non Pensioners ESM of all Ranks \n:- \n(a) Serious Diseases   as listed below: - \nAngioplasty, Angiography, CABG, \nOpen Heart Surgery, Valve \nReplacement, Pacemaker Implant, \nRenal Implant, Prostate Surgery, Joint \nReplacement and Cerebral Stoke. \n \nOther Diseases : Where more than         \nRs 1.00 Lac has been spent on \ntreatment  \n \n \n \n \n    75%/90% of total    \nexpenditure  \n    Officer and PBOR respectively  \nUpto Rs 1.25 Lac (max)  \n \n \n \n(b)    Dialysis and Cancer treatment     75%/90% of total expenditure   \n    Officer and PBOR respectively \nUpto a  max of Rs 75,000/- per FY \nonly. \n \n4. Prime Minister’s Scholarship Scheme:    Total 5500 scholarship are provided \nto eligible wards based on merit for the entire duration of the courses  from National \nDefence Fund (NDF). The rates of Scholarship are as follows : - \n \n(a)   Rs. 2500/- per month for boys. \n(b)   Rs. 3000/- per month for girls. \n \n \n \n...3/- \n \n\n: 3 : \n \n5. Financial support to institutions involved in rehabilitation of ESM :-     \n \nS. No. Organisation Quantum of Aid/Grant \n \n(a) Paraplegic Rehabilitation \nCenters \n(i) Kirkee \n(ii) Mohali \n \nEstt                     Inmates \ni) Rs 1.20 crore       30,000/-                       \nwef Apr 16            pa per \nii) Rs 10,00,000/-      inmate                           \n(wef Apr 2015)                            \n(b) All India Gorkha Ex -servicemen \nwelfare association, Dehradun \nRs. 12,00,000/- pa \n \n(c) \nCheshire Homes \n(i)   Lucknow \n(ii)  Dehradun  \n(iii) Delhi \n \nRs 9,000/- pa per inmate \n(d) War Memorial Hostels .  There \nare 36 WMHs which provide \nshelter to the children of War \nWidows/War disabled, \nattributable and non attributable \ncases. \nRs 1350/- per month \n \n6. Reservation of seats in Medical/Dental Colleges for wards of Defence \nPersonnel as Govt of India Nominee. A total of 35 MBBS seats and 3 seats in \nBDS courses are allotted by Ministry of Health Family Welfare to KSB for wards of \ndefence personnel as a Govt of India nominee. \n \n7. Reservation of seats  in Medical/Dental/Professional/ Non professional \nCourses by States/UTs/Central/State Universities/Autonomous Institutions:\n There are provisions of Reservation of Seats in  Medical/Dental/Professional/ \nNon professional Courses by States/U Ts/Central/State Universities /Autonomous \nInstitutions for wards/widows of ESMs as per the priority laid down by MoD.  \n \n8. Subsidised accommodation in Sainik Rest House:  There are Sainik Rest Houses \nspread all across the country, which is available at nominal costs. \n \n9. 75 percent concession in rail fare for travel in II Class is available to war widows. \nThis concession for War Widows can be availed on production of the I-Card issued \nby the KSB.  \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-B REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322 FOR ANSWER ON 11.12.2019 REGARDING \n‘WELFARE OF EX-SERVICEMEN’.  \nRESETTLEMENT SCHEMES ARE RUN BY DGR \nFollowing Ex -servicemen Resettlement schemes are run by DGR to provide \nresettlement opportunities to  Ex-servicemen of three Armed Forces Services.   \n(a)   Placement Assistance through on -line registration at DGR . – Directorate \nGeneral Resettlement (DGR) sponsors Ex -Servicemen to various Govt organizations, \nPublic Sector Undertakings, Corporate Houses, Private Sector, Central Para Military \nForces etc based on their requisition for re-employment of ESM.    \n(b)    DGR Sponsored Security Agency Scheme . – The DGR empanels / sponsors \nESM run Private Security Agencies , and State ESM Corporations for providing \nsecurity guards to various CPSUs, Corporate Houses, Private sector Undertakings etc.   \n(c)   ESM Coal Loading And Transportation Scheme.  – The Scheme is \nadministered on the basis of MoU between the Coal India Ltd (CIL) and DGR.  \n(d)   Management Of CNG Station By ESM In NCR.  -  Desirous ESM(O)s \nregistered in the scheme are sponsored on receipt of requisition from IGL (New \nDellhi) & MNGL (Pune).   \n(e) Allotment Of BPCL/IOCL COCO Outlets Pan India.  – ESM (Officer)/JCO \nwho is registered with DGR and has not availed any other benefit is sponsored for the \nCOCO outlets in the state of choice given by ESM (Officer) based on Oil Company’s \nrequisition.  \n(f) Allotment Of Mother Dairy Milk Booths And Fruit & Vegetable (Safal) \nShops In NCR. - The ESM register for Milk / SAFAL (Fruit and Vegetable \nbooths separately and give multiple choice of zones for the purpose of areas where \nbooths are required.  \n(g) Coal Tipper Attachment Scheme. – This scheme is linked with the ESM Coal \nloading and transportation scheme.   \n \n \n...2/- \n \n\n: 2 : \n \n(h) Tipper at tachment Scheme for War Widows and Disabled Soldiers . – \n War Widows upto age of 65 and disabled soldiers having 50% or more \ndisability are also allowed to enrol for this scheme.   \n(i) Allotment of Regular LPG Distributor Ship . –  War Widows/Dependents of \nthose who died in war, war disabled/disabled on duty while serving in operational \narea, widows/dependents of those who died in harness due to attributable or \naggravated causes to Military Servic e and ex -servicemen disabled in peace due to \nattributable or aggravated causes to Military Service are eligible.  \n (j) Retail Outlet Dealership (Petrol/Diesel) . – War Widows/Dependents of those \nwho died in war, war disabled/disabled on duty while serving i n operational area, \nwidows/dependents of those who died in harness due to attributable or aggravated \ncauses to Military Service and ex -servicemen disabled in peace due to attributable or \naggravated causes to Military Service are eligible. \n(k)   DGR ESM Emp loyment Seminars / Job Fairs . – A Memorandum of \nUnderstanding (MoU) was signed in 2014 between DGR and the Confederation of \nIndian Industry (CII) with the aim to generate employment opportunity for ESM in the \ncorporate sector.   Based on the MoU, ESM Job F airs have been organized with the \nhelp of CII. These Job Fairs are conducted at a suitable venue where the suitably \nidentified ESM are provided to the corporate employers based on the openings offered \nmatched with the skills of the ESM.   \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-C REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322 FOR ANSWER ON 11.12.2019 REGARDING \n‘WELFARE OF EX-SERVICEMEN’.  \nMEASURES INSTITUTED BY VARIOUS SERVICE HEADQUARTERS FOR  \nESM /WIDOWS \n \n1. Following measures have been taken by various Service Headquarters  :- \n \nS. No. SHQ Measures \n(a) Army  \nHeadquarters \n An exclusive Directorate viz. Dte of India n \nArmy Veterans (DIAV) have been formed at \nArmy  HQ  to assist Veterans in resolving their \nPensions and Welfare Issues.  \n \n(b) Air \nHeadquarters \n(i) An exclusive Directorate viz. Dte of Air \nVeterans (DAV) have been formed at Air HQ \nsince Sep 2012 to assist Air Veterans in \nresolving their Pensions and Welfare Issues.  \n \n(ii) IAF Placement Cell (IAFPC) have been \nfunctional at DAV for prov iding employment \nassistance to retired/ retiring Air Force Personnel \n \n(c) Naval \nHeadquarters  \nAn exclusive Directorate viz. Dte of Ex \nServicemen Affairs  (DESA) have been formed \nat Naval  HQ to assist Naval Veterans in \nresolving their Pensions and Welfare  Issues. It \nalso facilitates skill certification of retiring Naval \npersonnel. \n \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-D REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322 FOR ANSWER ON 11.12.2019 REGARDING \n‘WELFARE OF EX-SERVICEMEN’.  \nMAIN ACTIVITIES OF KENDRIYA SAINIK BOARD (KSB) SECTT. \n \n1. Organize and conduct following :- \n(a) The meeting of KSB and Director, DSW / Secy RSBs. \n \n(b) Annual Meeting of Management Committee of the Armed Forces \nFlag Day Fund. \n \n(c) Periodic meetings of the Executive Committee of the Armed Forces \nFlag Day Fund. \n \n2. Progressing implementation of the decisions taken with concerned agencies. \n \n3. Provide policy directive, as approved by the MoD, to Depts of Sainik Welfare \nin States / UTs on matters of welfare of Ex-Servicemen. \n \n4. Monitor and guide the Depts of Sainik Welfare in the States in their \nfunctioning as per guidelines laid down by KSB / Ministry of Defence. \n \n5. Provide budgetary support for establishment and maintenance costs of Depts \nof Sainik Welfare and Zila Sainik Welfare Offices to State / UTs. \n \n6. Attend as member of the Selection Committee convened invariably under the \nChief Secretary of the State for selection of Director, Dept of Sainik Welfare / \nSecretary RSB and Zila Sainik Welfare Officer / Secretary ZSB co nvened one month \nprior to the post falling vacant. \n \n7. Inspect the Department of Sainik Welfare of States / UTs each year and report \non their functioning to State Govt / MoD. \n \n8. Attend Rajya Sainik Board and Amalgamated Fund Meeting in States as \nspecial invitee. \n \n9. Administer Armed Forces Flag Day Fund (AFFDF). \n \n10. Deal with redressal and queries related to welfare of Ex -Servicemen and \nfamilies of deceased service personnel. \n \n11. Operate the Scheme for allotment of Medical, Dental and Engineering seats \nunder Ministry of Defence quota. \n \n \n \n...2/- \n \n\n: 2 : \n \n12. Organize and conduct ‘Armed Forces Flag Day Collection’ at Central Govt. \nDepts located at Delhi. \n \n13. Provide flags, posters and publicity material for conduct of the AFFD in States \n/ UTs and Indian Mission abroad. \n \n14. Operation of centrally sponsored schemes such as RMEWF (Raksha  Mantri \nEx-servicemen Welfare Fund) and PM Scholarship Schemes. \n \n15. Issue I-Card for availing Rail Travel Concession to War Widows and to ESM \nin special case. \n \n16. Provide financial assistance to paraplegic homes at PRC Kirkee  & Mohali, 35 \nWar Memorial Hostels, Cheshire Homes and various institutes and visit them to \ncheck its proper distribution. \n \n17. Advise on the correct investment of the Amalgamated Fund by the RS Bs of \nthe States / UTs. \n \n18. Inspect ZSBs at random to check their functioning and effectiveness. \n \nAll activities mentioned above  cater for providing financial assistance related \nto welfare of ESM /Widows/wards and to redressal  of grievances through 33 R ajya \nSainik Boards and 403 Zila Sainik Boards, across the country. \n \n \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_323": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 323 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2019 \n \nRAILWAY PROJECTS  \n \n†*323. SHRI BHAGIRATH CHOUDHARY:  \n \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) the details of new railway projects sanctioned in the State of Rajasthan \nduring the last five years, zone-wise and year-wise; \n  \n(b) the number of projects sanctioned during the last five years on which \nwork is going on at present along with project -wise details of expenditure \nincurred so far; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to sanction any new project in the \nState; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 323 BY SHRI BHAGIRATH CHOUD HARY TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 11.12.2019 REGARDING RAILWAY PROJECTS \n \n(a) and (b): Railway projects are not sanctioned State -wise, but Zone -wise, \nwhose jurisdiction cuts across the State boundaries. However, during the last \nfive years and current year (2014 -15 to 2018-19 and 2019-20), 04 new lines, 01 \ngauge conversion and 08 dou bling projects covering a total length of 1,772 km \nand costing `23,206 crore falling fully/partly in the State of Rajasthan were \nincluded in Railway Budget. The details of these projects are appended. \n \nDuring 2009 -14, 798 Km length (61 Km New Line, 675 Km Gauge \nConversion and 62 Km Doubling ) has been commissioned in the S tate of \nRajasthan at an average rate of 160 Km per year. \n \nDuring 2014 -19, 1516 Km (80 Km New Line, 483 Km Gauge Conversion \nand 953 Km Doubling) has been commissioned in the State of Rajasth an at an \naverage rate of 303 Km per year, which is 190% of commissioning during   \n2009-14. \n \nAverage Annual Budget allocati on for infrastructure projects and  safety \nworks, falling fully/partly in the State of Rajasthan, during 2014 -19 has been \nenhanced to `2951 crore per year,  from `682 crore per year (during 2009 -14). \nThus, the average annual budget allocation during 2014 -19 is 433% of average \nannual budget allocation of 2009-14 (`682 crore per year). \n \n(c) and (d): Sanction of projects for development of Railways is a continuous \nprocess. Railway projects are sanctioned, based on demands raised by State \nGovernments, Central Ministries, Members of Parliament, other public \nrepresentatives and Railways’ own requirement, participation by State \nGovernment or pri vate entities etc. They are taken up on the basis of \nremunerativeness, last mile connectivity, missing links and alternate routes \netc. \n*****  \n\n \n \nAPPENDIX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND (b) OF STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 323 BY SHRI BHAGIRATH CHOUDHARY TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON \n11.12.2019 REGARDING RAILWAY PROJECTS \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nProject \nYear of \ninclusion\n/ \nsanction \nin \nBudget \nZonal \nRailway \nLatest \nanticipated \ncost \n(` in crore) \nExp-\nenditure \nupto \nMarch \n2019 (` \nin crore) \nOutlay \nfor 2019-\n20 (` in \ncrore) \nStatus \nDoubling \n1. Nimach-\nChittaur\ngarh \n(55.73 \nKm) \n2015-16 Western \nRailway \n389.99 165.33 139 Shambhupura-\nChittaurgarh \n(13.10 km): \nCommissioned. \nWorks taken up \nin balance \nsection. \n2. Phulera- \nDegana \n(108 Km) \n2015-16 North \nWestern \nRailway \n686 4.43 150 Land \nacquisition \ntaken up. (14.70 \nHa  out of 27 Ha \nland has been \nacquired). \nWork taken up \nin available \nland. \n3. Mathura-\nJhansi \n3rd line \n(274 Km) \n2015-16 North \nCentral \nRailway \n3677.76 343 276 Land \nacquisition, \nforest/wild life \nclearance have \nbeen taken up. \nThere is an \nissue of forestry \nclearance in Taj \nTrapezium \nZone. Final \ndecision is \npending with \nHon’ble \nSupreme Court. \nWork taken up \nin available \nland. \n4. Degana-\nRai Ka \nBagh \n(145 Km) \n2018-19 North \nWestern \nRailway \n808.85 12.40 76 Work taken up \nin available \nland. \n  \n\n \n \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nProject \nYear of \ninclusion\n/ \nsanction \nin \nBudget \nZonal \nRailway \nLatest \nanticipated \ncost \n(` in crore) \nExp-\nenditure \nupto \nMarch \n2019 (` \nin crore) \nOutlay \nfor 2019-\n20 (` in \ncrore) \nStatus \n5. Agra \nFort-\nBandi \nkui ( 150 \nKm) \n2018-19 North \nCentral \nRailway \n1519.12 Expenditure is not incurred as  the \nProject has been included in Budget \n2018-19 subject to requisite \nGovernment clearances. However, an \noutlay of `0.10 crore has been \nprovided for this project for the year \n2019-20. \n6. Mathura-\nDholpur \n4th line \n(107 Km) \n2018-19 North \nCentral \nRailway \n2945.75 Expenditure is not incurred as  the \nProject has been included in Budget \n2018-19 subject to requisite \nGovernment clearances. However, an \noutlay of `0.10 crore has been \nprovided for this project for the year \n2019-20. \n7. Dhaulpur\n-Jhansi-\nBina 4th \nline  \n(321.80 \nKm) \n2018-19 North \nCentral \nRailway, \nWest \nCentral \nRailway \n4869.95 Expenditure is not incurred as  the \nProject has been included in the \nBudget 2018 -19 subject to requisite \nGovernment clearances. However, an \noutlay of `0.10 crore has been \nprovided for this project for the year \n2019-20. \n8. Sawai \nMadhopur\n-Jaipur-\nDL \n(131.27 \nKm) \n2019-20 North \nWestern \nRailway \n946.13 Expenditure is not incurred as  the \nProject has been included in the \nBudget 2019 -20 subject to requisite \nGovernment clearances. However, an \noutlay of `0.001 crore has been \nprovided for this project for the year \n2019-20. \nGauge Conversion \n1. Marwar-\nMavli \n(152 Km) \n2017-18 North \nWestern \nRailway \n2654.03 Expenditure is not incurred as  the \nProject included in budget 2017 -18 \nand has been decided to be retained \nas metre gauge Heritage Line.  \nHowever, an outlay of `0.10 crore has \nbeen provided for this project for the \nyear 2019-20. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nProject \nYear of \ninclusion\n/ \nsanction \nin \nBudget \nZonal \nRailway \nLatest \nanticipated \ncost \n(` in crore) \nExp-\nenditure \nupto \nMarch \n2019 (` \nin crore) \nOutlay \nfor 2019-\n20 (` in \ncrore) \nStatus \nNew line \n1. Ajmer (Nasira- \nbad)-Sawai \nMadhopur \n(Chauth Ka \nBarwara) Via \nTonk (165 Km) \n2015-16 North \nWestern \nRailway \n1,980.21 Expenditure is not incurred as  \nthe Project was sanctioned \nwith the proviso to share 50% \ncost of the project  and \nprovision of free land by State \nGovernment of Rajasthan. State \nGovernment refused to share \nthe cost of the project. \nProject is held up on this \naccount. However, On the \nrequest of Government of \nRajasthan, bankability study has \nbeen initiated for the project. \nHowever, an outlay of `0.20 \ncrore has been provided for this \nproject for the year 2019-20. \n2. Nimach-Choti \nSadri-Bari \nSadri (48 Km) \n2017-18 Western \nRailway \n495.18 Expenditure is not incurred as  \nthe Project sanctioned in \nOctober 2018. Final Location \nSurvey (FLS) has been taken up. \nHowever, an outlay of `10 crore \nhas been provi ded for this \nproject for the year 2019-20. \n3. Taranga Hill -\nAbu Road via \nAmbaji (89 Km) \n2017-18 North \nWestern \nRailway \n1879.15 Expenditure is not incurred as  \nthe Project has been included in \nthe Budget subject to requisite \nGovernment clearances.  \nHowever, an outlay of `0.10 \ncrore has been provided for this \nproject for the year 2019-20. \n4. Gudha-\nThathana-\nMithri-\nConstruction \nof dedi- \ncated test \ntrack for \ntesting and \ntrial of rolling \nstocks and \ninfrastruc ture \ncompo- \nnents (25 Km) \n2018-19 North \nWestern \nRailway \n353.48 Expenditure is not incurred as  \nthe New work has been \nsanctioned in 2018 -19. \nPreliminary activities have been \ntaken up. However, an outlay of \n`0.10 crore has been provided \nfor this project for the year \n2019-20. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_324": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 324 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11 th DECEMBER, 2019 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11 th DECEMBER, 2019 \nINDIA’S SHARE IN GLOBAL TRADE *324. SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL: SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI: Will the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वािण�य एवं उ�ोग मं�ी ) be pleased to state: pleased to state: (a) whether a high -level advisory group constituted to look at ways to increase India’s share in global trade has submitted its report to the Government; (b) if so, the detailed recommendations made in the report; (c) whether the group has suggested taking steps towards reforming labour laws, tax system, easing entry rules and doing away with protectionist policies; (d) if so, the follow up action taken/ being taken by the Government on the recommendations of the group to increase the share of trade; and (e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government for reforms in different sectors following the corporate tax reforms? \nANSWER okf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=h ( Jh ih;w’k xks;y )THE MINISTER OF  COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n(a) to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. *****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 324   FOR ANSWER ON 11 th DECEMBER,2019 REGARDING “INDIA’S SHARE IN GLOBAL TRADE ”.(a) Yes, Sir. (b) and (c) : Detailed recommendations of the HLAG are available on the website of the Department of Commerce ( https://commerce.gov.in ). (d) The suggesti ons and recommendations of the report of the HLAG have been shared with the   different departments concerned .(e) As regards reforms in tax system, it is stated that through Taxation Laws (Amendment) Ordinance, 2019 (the Ordinance), corporate tax rates have been reduced to 22% plus applicable surcharge and cess for all domestic corporate persons not claiming any deduction or incentive.  Further, an option has been given to any new domestic deduction or incentive.  Further, an option has been given to any new domestic manufacturing companies incorporated on or after 01.10.2019 to avail lower corpora te tax rates of 15% with applicable surcharge and cess without claiming any deduction or incentive as per sub -section (2) of the section 115BAB of the Act, provided they commence manufacturing or production of an article or thing by 31.03.2023. Government has already carried out a number of reforms in various sectors and the reform process is a continuous process for the improvement in the economy. Some of these are by way of amendments in various acts such as the Finance (amendment) Bills, The Special Economic Zones (Amendment) Bill, Amendments in the Goods and Services Tax Act, the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code ,etc.   ***** ***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_325": "Contd…2/\n-\n \n \n-\n \n2 \n-\n \n \nInterest Subvention Scheme (ISS) was launched in the year 2006\n-\n07 with a view to \nprovide concessional s\nhort term crop loans including loan availed through Kisan Credit Card \n(KCC) to the farmers. Interest subvention of 2% and prompt repayment incentive of 3% on \nrestructured crop loans is given to farmers affected by severe natural calamities. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nThe Government is already providing urea to farmers at subsidized rates. Urea is \nbeing provided to the farmers at a statutorily notified Maximum Retail Price (MRP). The MRP \nof 45 kg bag of urea is Rs.242 per bag (exclusive of charges towards neem coating a\nnd \ntaxes as applicable) and the MRP of 50 kg bag of urea is Rs.268 per bag (exclusive of \ncharges towards neem coating and taxes as applicable). The difference between the \ndelivered cost of urea at farm gate and net market realization by the urea units is g\niven as \nsubsidy to the urea manufacturer/importer by the Government of India. The Government of \nIndia is also granting subsidy on P&K fertilizers under the Nutrient Based Subsidy scheme. \nUnder the policy, the subsidy on P&K fertilizers is announced by the \nGovernment on an \nannual basis before the beginning of each financial year. The Subsidy is announced for each \nnutrient i.e Nitrogen (N), Phosphorus (P), Potash (K) and Sulphur (S) on per kg basis, which \nis converted into subsidy per tonne depending upon the\n \nnutrient content in each grade of \nthese fertilizers. MRP (Maximum Retail Price) of P&K fertilizers has been allowed to be fixed \nby manufactures/ importers at reasonable level. Presently, NBS Policy is applicable on 21 \nGrades of P&K fertilizers.\n \n******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_326": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 326 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2019 \n \nFUTURE OF MINING \n \n \n*326. SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA: \n \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the future of mining in our country appears uncertain due to the legal \nframework, resource overuse, environmental concerns, etc.; and  \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and the manner in which the Government plans to address \nthe above and other related issues?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n \n \n(a) to (b): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n  \n\nTHE STATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 326 REGARDING “FUTURE OF MINING” RAISED BY SHRI \nBHAGWANTH KHUBA, FOR REPLY ON 11.12.2019. \n \n(a) and (b):- No, Sir. The Central Government has taken various steps for development \nof mining sector in the country. The Mines and Mineral (Development and Regulation) \n(MMDR) Act, 1957 was amended w.e.f. 12.01.2015 and auction has been introduced as \na method of allocation of mining leases to bring greater transparency in the mining \nsector. The Cent ral Government has made certain other amendments and works \ntogether with the State Governments for effective implementation of the Act and the \nRules.  \n \nThe Central Government has also formulated National Mineral Policy, 2019 for a \nvibrant and forward looking mineral sector.  \n \nConsidering the future of mining industry National Mineral Exploration Trust has \nbeen constituted under Section 9(C) of the MMDR Act for taking up detailed and \nregional exploration and studies for mineral development. Geological Survey  of India \n(GSI) and Mineral Exploration Corporation Limited (MECL) have  been entrusted the \nwork of exploration in mining sector. Various Central and State Government agencies \nhave been notified under section 4 of MMDR Act for undertaking exploration.  \n \nThe Central Government has also amended the Mineral Conservation and \nDevelopment Rules, 2017 and the concept of star rating and the period to achieve the \nstar rating have been modified considering the problem s faced by the medium and \nsmall mine owners.  \n \n \n***** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_327": "������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n������������������������ �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n�������������������������������������������� �\n�\n����������������������������� ��\n�\n� �������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n�� �� ��������������� �������������������������������������� �������\n��������� ��������� �������� ���� �������� ������������ ������\n��������������������������� �\n�\n�� �� �������� ������ ��� ���� �������� ����� ������ ��������� ����\n�����������������������������������������������������������\n���������� �\n�\n�� �� ���������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ������ ��� ������ ����� ���� ������� ���� ������� ���������\n��������������������������� ��\n�\n�\n�\n������ �\n�\n�������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������������������� �\n�\n�\n�� �� ������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n����� �\n\n�\n���������� ��������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ������ ���� ��� ���� ���\n�������������������������������������������������������\n��� ��������� ��� ���� ������ ��� ����������� ����������\n������������������������������������ �������� �\n�\n�\n������������ ���� ����� ����� ������ ����� ���� ���� ��� ���� ���� ������\n������������� �� ������ ������� ��� ������ ����������� ����� �����\n���������������������� �������� ���������������� ������������ �������� ������������ ���������������������� �������������� ���\n�������� ������������ ������� ������� �� �������� ����\n������������������� ���� ��������� ��������� ���� ���� ������ ��������\n������������ ��\n�\n���� � �������������������������\n�������������������� �\n���� ��� � ���� �\n���� ��� � ��� �\n���� ��� � ���� �\n���� ��� � �� �\n���� ����������\n����������� �\n����� �\n����� � ����� �\n� � ���������������� �������� �\n�\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ����������� ����������� ���� ����� ��� ������ ��� ���� ����\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n������� ��� ����� ����������� ����� ������ ����� ����� �����\n����������� ��� ���� ����� ���� ������������ ��� �������\n����������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n����� �",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_328": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n328\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19\nTH \n \nNOVEMBER, 2019\n \n \nINTRODUCTION OF ZERO BUDGET FARMING\n \n \n328. \n \nSHRI KIRTI VARDHAN SINGH: \n \nSHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether\n \nthe Ministry is contemplating the introduction of Zero Budget Natural Farming \n(ZBNF) to enhance the financial position of Indian farmers, if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(b)\n \n \nthe budgetary allocation for ZBNF; \n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether sections of Agricultural scientis\nts, as per newspaper reports, are opposed to \nZBNF as it is not cost effective, if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether there is any scientific evidence regarding the efficacy of ZBNF, if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government is positive that ZBNF will aid in doubling the income of \nfarmers by 2022, if so, the details of all such schemes that will help achieve the target set?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(\na)\n \nto \n(\ne\n)\n:\n \nGovernment has been promoting organic farming under the dedicated scheme \nof Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY) which  encourages all kinds of chemical free \nfarming systems including Zero budget Natural Farmin\ng\n. The budget allocation for PKVY for \n2019\n-\n20 is Rs. 325 crores. \nIndian Council of Agriculture Research through ICAR\n-\nIndian \nInstitute of Farming Systems Research has initiated a study on Evaluation of Zero Budget \nNatural Farming practices in Basmati/ coars\ne rice\n-\nwheat system from Rabi Season 2017 at 4 \nlocations namely Modipuram, Pantnagar, Ludhiyana, Kurukshetra .\n \n \n \nNational Academy of Agricultural Sciences (NAAS) has also organized a \nbrainstorming season on Zero Budget Natural Farming \nin August\n, 2019.\n \n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n \n\n \n-\n \n2 \n-\n \n  \n \nGovernment has\n \nbeen implementing several schemes aiming at meeting the target of \ndoubling of \nfarmer’s\n \nincome by \n2022. \nThe major schemes include:\n \n \n\n \nNational Food Security Mission \n–\n \nfor improved production and productivity of food \ngrains\n \nand oilseeds.\n \n \n\n \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture(MIDH)\n-\n \nto enhance production and \nproductivity of horticultural crops\n \n \n\n \nParamparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana and  Mission organic Value Chain Development for \nNorth East Region \n–\nto reduce cost of cultivation and ensure chemical free quality \nproducts that can fetch premium prices to farmers \n \n \n\n \nSoil health Cards\n-\n \nAims at promotion of \njudicious use of fertilisers through soil test \nbased recommendations there by reducing excess use of fertilisers \n \n \n\n \nPMKSY\n-\nPDMC (Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana\n-\nPer Drop More Crop)\n-\n  \nto \npromote  water conservation  and reduction in use of  inputs throug\nh drip and sprinkler \nirrigation\n \n \n\n \nBamboo Mission and  Agro forestry\n \n \n\n \ne\n-\nNAM \n–\n \nfor \ntransparent competitive markets for improved remuneration to the \nfarmers.\n \n \n\n \nCrop loans\n-\nInterest Subvention Scheme (ISS) , Kisan Credit Card(K CC)\n \n \n\n \nAssured Prices\n-\nMinimum Support\n \nPrice (MSP)\n \n \n\n \nPradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (\nPMKISAN)\n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_329": "��\n�\n������������������� �\n��������������������������� �\n�������������� ������������������ �\n�\n���������� �\n�������������������� ������\n������������������ �������������� �������\n�\n����������������������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� ���� ������������������ ����������� ��� ���� ����������� ��� �������� ���� ����������\n�������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n� �������������������������������� ������������� �\n�������������������������������������� �\n������������������������� ��\n�\n�\n����������� ������������������������������������������������\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n� �\n\n��\n�\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������\n������ ����������� ��� ���� ������ �������� ��������� ���� � �������� ���� �\n����� ��� ��������������������������������������� �����\n�\n���� �������� ���������������� ���������������� ������������ ������ ������������ �������������� ���������� ����������������� ��� ����� ��\n������ ��� ������ ���� ������������ ��� �������� �������� ��������\n���������� ��� ������ ������ ����������� ���� ��������� ����������������� �������� ����\n�������� ������ ��� ���� ����������� ��� �������� ����� ������� ���� ���� �������� ������ ��� ����\n������������������������������������������\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������� ����\n�\n���� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� ���������������� �������������� �������� �������� ���������������� ������ �����