ment plant available? \n7 Covered storm water drainage? \n8 100% paved roads available inside the park? \n9 Operational Street lighting available inside the park? \n10 Does the park operator adopt use of captive renewable energy generation \n(solar/wind etc.)? \n11 Availability of gas pipeline? \n \n2. External Infrastructure and Connectivity \nS.No. Parameter \n12 Logistics terminal in proximity of 20 Km? \n13 Power sub-station availability? \n14 Solid waste disposal/treatment site available in proximity of 10 Km? \n15 Bus Stop available inside the park? \n16 Distance from nearest rail head (Km) <20 Km \n17 Distance from nearest airport (Km) <100Km \n18 Distance from nearest sea port (Km) <200Km \n19 Distance from nearest major city (Km) <50Km \n \n3. Business Services and Facilities \nS.No. Parameter \n20 Are plot availability and price details available on SIDC website? \n21 Is the procedure for application & allotment of land implemented online? \n22 Single Window Services provided by the park? \n23 Dormitory for labor and drivers available in the park? \n24 Bank branch/ ATM available in the park? \n25 Canteen/ Restaurant available in the park? \n26 Weighbridge for cargo and freight available in proximity of 1 Km? \n27 Designated truck parking available within the park? \n28 Skill development center within the park?  \n \n4. Environment and Safety Management \nS.No Parameter \n29 Fire hydrants/fire trucks/fire-fighting systems available in the park? \n30 Disaster management plan/protocols established for park safety? \n31 Air Quality monitoring station available in the park? \n32 Functional CCTV and other security system available for the park premises? \n33 Rest area for women workers available in the park or its vicinity? \n\n34 PHC/CSC/ESI Dispensary/25 Bed Hospital inside the park? \n*******  ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_465": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.465\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.11.2019\nVISIT BY DIGNITARIES\n465. Dr Amol Ramsing Kolhe:\n          Shri Sunil Dattaray T atkare:\n          Dr. Subhash Ramrao Bhamre:\n          Shri Kuldeep Rai Sharma:\n          Shrimati Supriya Sule:\n          Shri AKP Chinraj:\nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  the  details  of  foreign  trips  undertaken  by  the  Prime  Minister  of\nIndia,  External  Affairs  Minister  and  other  dignitaries  during  each  of  the\nlast three months and the current month, country-wise;\n(b) the  details  of  foreign  dignitaries  who  visited  India  during  the  last\nthree months and the current months, country-wise;\n(c) the  total  amount  of  funds  spent  by  the  Government  on  the  said  visits;\n(d)   the  areas  identified  for  co-operation  and  discussions  held  during  the\nsaid visits;\n(e) the  agreements  and  MoU  signed  and  the  advantages  to  the  country  as  a\nresult thereof;\n(f) the  details  of  the  benefits  accrued  to  India  by  visiting  these\ncountries; and\n(g) the  steps  taken/being  taken  by  the  Government  to  boost  the  bilateral\nrelations with these foreign countries?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN]\n(a) Information is placed at Annexure 1\n(b) Information is placed at Annexure 2\n(c) Information is placed at Annexure 3\n(e)  Information is placed at Annexure 4\n(d), (f) and (g) High level visits are an important means by which nations serve their\nnational  interests  and  implement  their  foreign  policy  objectives.  Visits  by  dignitaries\nenable  enhanced  understanding  of  India’s  perspectives  on  regional  and  global  issues\namong foreign partners at the highest level. \nThese visits and the understandings reached therein, enable India to  strengthen\nrelations  with  partner  countries  across  a  wide  range  of  sectors  including  trade  &\ninvestment,  technology  and  defence  collaboration.  Such  outcomes  also  contribute  to\nIndia’s national development agenda to promote economic growth and well being of our\npeople. \nAt the multilateral level,  India is increasingly  contributing to shaping the global\nagenda on an array of cross-cutting issues such as climate change, trans-national crime &\nterrorism, cyber security & nuclear non-proliferation, while also offering  the world, India’s\n1\n\nown unique solutions to global partners for addressing global issues, like the International\nSolar Alliance and the Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure.  \n***\n2\n\nAnnexure 1\nVisits by President, Vice President, Prime Minister, External Affairs Minister and \nMOS for External Affairs (Aug– Nov 2019) \nDignitary Countries visited Period of visit\n1 President Benin, The Gambia and Guinea 28 July - 3 Aug 2019\n2 President Iceland, Switzerland and Slovenia 9-17 Sept 2019 \n3 President Philippines and Japan 17-23 Oct 2019 \n4 Vice President Lithuania, Latvia and Estonia 17-22 Aug 2019\n5 Vice President Comoros and Sierra Leone 10-14 Oct 2019 \n6 Vice President Azerbaijan 25-26 Oct 2019 \n7 Prime Minister Bhutan 17  – 18 Aug 2019\n8 Prime Minister France, UAE and Bahrain 22  – 27 Aug 2019\n9 Prime Minister Russia 4 -6 Sept 2019\n10 Prime Minister USA 21 – 28 Sept 2019\n11 Prime Minister Saudi Arabia 28-29 Oct 2019 \n12 Prime Minister Thailand 2-4 Nov 2019 \n13 Prime Minister Brazil 13-14 Nov 2019 \n14\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Thailand 1-2 Aug 2019 \n15\nExternal Affairs \nMinister China 11-13 Aug 2019\n16\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Bangladesh and Nepal 21-22 Aug 2019\n17\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Hungary, Russia and Poland  25-31 Aug 2019 \n18\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Maldives 3-4 Sept 2019\n19\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Indonesia and Singapore 4-10 Sept 2019 \n20\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Finland 19-21 Sept 2019\n21\nExternal Affairs \nMinister USA 22 Sept - 2 Oct 2019\n22\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Azerbaijan 23-26 Oct 2019 \n23\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Thailand 2-4 Nov 2019 \n24 External Affairs Serbia 7-9 Nov 2019 \n3\n\nMinister\n25\nExternal Affairs \nMinister Netherlands 9-11 Nov 2019 \n26\nExternal Affairs \nMinister France 11-12 Nov 2019 \n27\nMOS for External \nAffairs \nPeru, Dominican Republic and Argentina 22-29 Aug 2019 \n28 MOS for External Affair Cameroon, Togo and Cote d'Ivoire 3-6 Sept 2019\n29\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nKuwait and Iraq  14-17 Sept 2019\n30\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nUSA 22-28 Sept 2019 \n31\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nSweden 2-4 Oct 2019\n32\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nVatican 12-13 Oct 2019 \n33\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nZambia and UAE (Dubai) 14-18 Oct 2019 \n34\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nReunion Islands 23-24 Oct 2019 \n35\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nUAE 6-7 Nov 2019\n36\nMOS for External \nAffairs\nKazakhstan 11-12 Nov 2019 \n4\n\nAnnexure 2\nVisits to India by dignitaries (Aug – Nov 2019)\nDignitary Period of visit \n1 Mr. Edgar Chagwa Lungu, President of Zambia 20-22 Aug 2019\n2 Dr.  Ralph  E.  Gonsalves,   Prime  Minister  of  St.  Vincent  and  the\nGrenadines\n8-12 Sept 2019\n3 Mr. Charles Peter David, Foreign Minister of Grenada 11-18 Sept 2019\n4 Mr. Khaltmaagiin Battulga, President of Mongolia 19-23 Sept 2019\n5 Sheikh Hasina Prime Minister of  Bangladesh 3-6 Oct 2019\n6 Mr. Julian Ventura Valero, Deputy Foreign Minister of Mexico 7 Oct 2019 \n7 Mr. Don Pramudwinai, Foreign Minister of Thailand 9-11 Oct 2019 \n8 Mr. Xi Jinping, President of  China 11-12 Oct 2019\n9 King Willem-Alaxender of Netherlands 13-18 Oct 2019\n10 Mr. Denis Moncada Colindres, Foreign Minister of Nicaragua 27-29 Oct 2019\n11 Mr. Delsy Rodriguez, Vice President of Venezuela 30 Oct - 2 Nov 2019\n12 Dr. Angela Merkel, Chancellor of Germany 31 Oct – 2 Nov 2019\n13 Mr. Pekka Haavisto, Foreign Minister of Finland 4-7 Nov 2019\n14 Prince of Wales